Debian 13 update (2026-10-01)
----------------------------
See QUALIFICATION-DEBIAN13 for the native amd64/arm64 package qualification.
Ubuntu-only scope statements below describe the initial packaging checkpoint.

Qore ZeroMQ module packages
==========================

This testing snapshot includes the native module, compiler metadata, HTML
reference, example tests and debug symbols. Full CLIENT/SERVER and RADIO/DISH
draft APIs are required; ordinary distribution ZeroMQ builds lack them.

Two pinned orig components contain libzmq 4.3.5 and CZMQ's recorded development
revision (preserving the existing macOS build choice without tracking master).
Their sources and licenses are recorded in vendor-sources.json. The repack
helper validates SHA256 and retains the listed build sources/templates only.
Prepare the primary orig from tracked module files excluding debian, download
the two archives outside the build, and run:

  debian/repack-dependencies.py ARCHIVE_DIRECTORY OUTPUT_DIRECTORY

Extract each component without its upstream wrapper into libzmq/ or czmq/,
copy debian, and run dpkg-buildpackage -S -sa -us -uc. dpkg-source -x restores
the components. Increment the package version before each distinct upload.

Build unprivileged without network using packaged Qore 3 and declared system
libraries. Private static ZeroMQ/CZMQ archives are included only in the module;
their public headers/libraries/tools are not installed. ELF archive symbols are
hidden to avoid interposing Qore or other modules. Shared system libsodium,
GnuTLS, libbsd, LZ4 and UUID are used where required. CURVE and draft/proxy
capability checks prevent silently reduced test coverage.

Five profiles (full, -B, -A, nocheck, nodoc), changed path/job-count repeat builds,
installed runtime/fresh qcc tests, metadata/docs/debug artifacts and SDK-free
install/purge/reinstall must pass. Only Ubuntu 26.04 amd64 / Qore SDK .12 is
initially qualified. No older package is available for upgrade qualification.
Cross builds fail explicitly because build tools execute Qore. Other releases,
architectures and the Qore ABI freeze remain general-release requirements.
Private dependencies require security monitoring and component updates.
Testing PPA publication requires signing and the matching published SDK.
